On 2008-03-14, Keith Davies <keith.davies@kjdavies.org> wrote:
> Janis <janis_papanagnou@hotmail.com> wrote:
>> to weaken the resistance for other (physical) types of damage. One
>> can, of course, say that elementals (as opposed to demons and wizards)
>> do _physical_ damage (not damage done by magic), but since this is a
>> game and fantasy and magic hardly explainable in real life terms it's
>> still an acceptable alternative; at least there wasn't the necessity
>> to change the whole resistance concept to just "fix" the elementals.
>
> Interesting idea.  It sounds like you're suggesting that fire resistance
> (the normal one) works against mundane fire.  You're okay against lava,
> fire traps, and the elemental plane of fire (*natural* effect -- anyone
> unprotected might take damage every round), but fireball, wands of fire,
> and fire elementals are still a worry.

This is a sensible place to split it (ie. is not just "fire elementals"
who can violate the model), but falls into one problem, which is that
you care a lot more about the second level than the first, barring the
elemental plane of fire's constant damage effect (which hearkens back to
the days when entering Gehennom without fire resistance killed you).

> I could live with that.  If there is a second level of protection that
> offers resistance (ignore a certain percentage, or flat amount, or
> cumulative amount) of magical fire damage that could work too.  The
> intrinsic might protect you against mundane fire, but a =oFireRes
> actually *does* offer you more than the intrinsic.  I could see potions
> of fire resistance, scrolls or spells of fire resistance, and so on.

If people were concerned about beankeeping before, adding timed
resistances is probably not a good way to go.  ;)  I know this would
mostly just piss people off if the sources in question weren't
absolutely littering the dungeon; forcing people to swap rings out for
every dragon they counter is _also_ aggravating, especially in places
where there are multiple dragons.

As I noted in my earlier message, there are solutions.  But just having
fire elementals punch through the current fire resistance model isn't 
a good one; it would take a more coherent split, which ends up being
overhauling the entire resistance model again (now there are two types
of fire, "fire" and "magic fire").

